WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.500
- Welcome back everybody.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.050
Next week marks the start of Passover

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.300
the major Jewish holiday
that celebrates freedom,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.030
after hundreds of years of enslavement.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.040
It's coming at a time when
there's serious concern,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.850
within the Jewish community
between antisemitic comments,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.760
from high profile figures and
the rise in vicious attacks.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.410
So many are living in fear,
which is in just a shame.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.420
My next guest is a young TikTok star,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.190
whose dance moves helped her rack up,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.800
over 12 million followers on social media.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.720
Now she's actually using
her global platform,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.360
to spread awareness and
combat antisemitism.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.571
We call that a rad human.

00:00:34.571 --> 00:00:37.321
(cheerful music)

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.904
Everybody, please welcome Montana Tucker.

00:00:41.904 --> 00:00:43.094
(crowd cheers)

00:00:43.094 --> 00:00:45.408
- Hi, thank you for having me.

00:00:45.408 --> 00:00:46.318
- You're welcome.

00:00:46.318 --> 00:00:48.120
You have the greatest
name ever, by the way.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.220
- Thank you so much, it is
my birth name, I promise.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.470
- Montana Tucker is the coolest name ever.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:53.400
- Thank you, thanks.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.200
- I know to mama she's here with us today,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.130
but that's a cool name.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.240
So how did I have to ask,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.640
how did you become an advocate
for the Jewish community?

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.200
- Yes, so I started off making,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.422
these really happy fun dance videos

00:01:03.422 --> 00:01:06.481
and with the recent rise in anti-Semitism,

00:01:06.481 --> 00:01:08.550
I knew that I wanted
to do something bigger

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.290
and deeper with my platform.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.150
And I am a proud Jewish woman.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.580
Both of my grandparents
are Holocaust survivors.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.424
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:01:15.424 --> 00:01:16.257
(crowd applauds)

00:01:16.257 --> 00:01:17.400
- Thank you, thank you.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.966
And I always heard the
stories my whole life.

00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:21.480
My grandparents used to
speak at all their schools

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.000
and all the schools, and it
was always important for them,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.050
education, education.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.330
So my Zaide grandfather,

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:28.770
he passed away three and a half years ago,

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.600
but he used to wear a pin
all the time that said,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.970
never forget, never again, I'm a survivor.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.430
So I knew that now this is my turn,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.770
this is my turn to make sure
that people never forget.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.140
- Absolutely, what a beautiful legacy,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.220
to keep reminding everyone.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.537
So what was the response when
you started kind of shifting?

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:47.430
- So when I first posted
just a lighthearted,

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.630
photo of my grandparents, the
comments were so shocking.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.210
I don't even want to repeat
some of them on live TV,

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.725
but a lot of them were,

00:01:55.725 --> 00:01:58.380
I shared that my grandparents
were Holocaust survivors

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.280
and that I was Jewish.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.200
And people said, oh of
course you're Jewish

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.610
and making everything about yourself

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.636
or the Holocaust never existed.

00:02:04.636 --> 00:02:07.680
I mean, how could people say
the Holocaust never existed?

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.657
- Well, some people still think
the planet earth is flat so.

00:02:10.657 --> 00:02:12.870
- It's mind boggling to me.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:13.713
- Keep walking.

00:02:13.713 --> 00:02:14.970
- [Montana] Keep walking,
where are you gonna go?

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.200
- Find the end.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.033
- Seriously.

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:17.866
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:17.866 --> 00:02:20.093
- And so I knew that, you know,

00:02:20.093 --> 00:02:23.820
I had this opportunity now to educate.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:24.653
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:24.653 --> 00:02:25.920
- [Montana] And over 60, there's a survey,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.200
that said over 60% of millennials,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.510
didn't know that over 6 million Jews,

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.010
were killed in the Holocaust.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.360
- How do they not learn that in school?

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.310
I remember learning that in school alone.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.320
- Well, apparently not
all states in the US,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.390
require Holocaust education.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:40.230
- Wow.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.344
- [Montana] Yeah.

00:02:41.344 --> 00:02:43.290
- I'm so blessed, I was at a school,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.270
that literally took us to Dachau,

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.590
I think is how you pronounce it.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.820
Yeah and it was, I encourage every school,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.160
to do every kind of, we were, you know,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.960
not a rich city where I'm from,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.180
and we had to like raise money to go,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.410
but when we went overseas,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:02.040
it makes the most impact to a child,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.830
to see something like
that and go, wait, what?

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:05.850
Like this happened?

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.860
And you walk through everything.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.480
It moves you in such a different way.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.630
And I think people that
have never experienced that,

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.400
sometimes people can have a hard time,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.290
even believing it was real, right?

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.270
Because it's so horrfic.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.100
- That's why like,
people need to see this,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.266
people need to experience it.

00:03:21.266 --> 00:03:22.099
- [Kelly] That's why
the pin was important,

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:22.932
that tour Grandfather wore, yeah.

00:03:22.932 --> 00:03:24.630
- And even if you don't
have the opportunity,

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.930
to go to a camp which I
hope that everyone can,

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:28.763
but if you don't have.

00:03:28.763 --> 00:03:29.596
- Even Googling at this point,

00:03:29.596 --> 00:03:31.146
like whenever I was young
they didn't have like,

00:03:31.146 --> 00:03:33.300
don't have the option,
I'm a lot older than you.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.410
There wasn't the option of like,

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.960
now you can literally get
online and see so much

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.610
and there's so much information out there.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.860
- And unfortunately there's
still so much doubt.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.180
- [Kelly] And so much misinformation.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.013
- Yeah.

00:03:43.013 --> 00:03:43.846
- That is a problem as well.

00:03:43.846 --> 00:03:45.240
Well, you were inspired
to take a trip to Poland.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.440
So tell us about that trip.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.570
- Yes, so there's another study that says,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.880
over 90% of the Jews
in Poland were killed,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.410
during World War II.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.600
My grandma is a survivor of Auschwitz,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.151
which was a camp in Poland.

00:03:56.151 --> 00:03:56.984
- Yeah.

00:03:56.984 --> 00:03:58.920
- And so I knew that I
always wanted to go there.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.810
My mom and I talked about
going there for years,

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.280
because again hearing my
grandparents' stories,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.703
my whole life but never really
had the opportunity to go.

00:04:04.703 --> 00:04:07.200
And I had a good friend, Israel Shater,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.300
who was like you know what,
we're gonna have you go.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.520
And I knew it was gonna be the absolute,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.200
hardest experience in my life.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.033
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:14.866
- But the most important

00:04:14.866 --> 00:04:16.650
and I knew it would make
my grandparents so proud.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.500
- Yeah, what a cool thing to
do with your platform, man.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.333
That's so cool.

00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:21.166
- Thank you.

00:04:21.166 --> 00:04:23.880
- Well, and you turn that trip
into a documentary, right?

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:24.713
That's what we're talking about.

00:04:24.713 --> 00:04:27.048
- Yes, a docu-series called
"How to Never Forget".

00:04:27.048 --> 00:04:29.280
We made it solely for social media.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.280
So it's available on all of
my social media platforms.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.113
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:33.113 --> 00:04:36.060
- And you know, yes, this
story is my story, right?

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.110
It's about my grandparents,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.110
it's about my ancestors, my
people, the Jewish people.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.910
But I knew if I brought
all of my followers

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.470
and also millions of people,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:45.330
that may not follow me along with me.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:46.163
- Yeah.

00:04:46.163 --> 00:04:47.519
- This would now become their story also.

00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:50.850
And hopefully all together
we can combat anti-Semitism

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:51.780
and all forms of hate.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.748
- Yeah, absolutely, information is key.

00:04:54.748 --> 00:04:55.581
(crowd applauds)

00:04:55.581 --> 00:04:56.970
And so Montana you told
us about your trip,

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.860
with your mom when you went to Poland.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.630
So it's part of the documentary, right?

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.880
- Yes, so this clip that
you're about to see,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.430
it is my mom and I standing in Auschwitz,

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.949
which is the camp that my
grandma is a survivor of.

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:11.610
We stand on tracks where
over a million Jews,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.200
were taken millions of people,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.540
but over a million Jews
were taken to their death.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.900
We stand, it's a very,
very emotional piece.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.654
We stand where my mother,

00:05:20.654 --> 00:05:23.340
I'm sorry my grandmother
last saw her mother.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.990
And you're gonna see it in this next clip.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.040
It's very emotional.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.050
- All right, well let's
take a look everybody.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.690
- I think that your great grandma knew,

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.180
she was going to be marched to her death.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.580
- [Montana] My mom tells
me the horrific story,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.580
of my 13 year old grandma and her mother,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.220
being put in the line for death,

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.650
while her sister was
put in the other line,

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.140
right here on these tracks.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.360
My grandma tried to stay with her mom,

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.530
but her mom had told her,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.010
whatever happens you stay
with your older sister.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.110
- That saved her life.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:56.280
- [Montana] The line she left,

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.200
went right to the gas chambers.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.910
I feel like I would've ran
to the other line and said,

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:01.590
"take me with you".

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.963
I wouldn't have been
able to not go with you.

00:06:05.936 --> 00:06:08.580
- Montana's mama Michelle's
actually in the audience,

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:10.320
with us today, hi Michelle.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.153
- Hi Kelly.

00:06:11.153 --> 00:06:12.580
- [Kelly] Thank you for joining us.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.856
- Thank you for having me.

00:06:14.856 --> 00:06:18.420
- So how did you feel
when you were sharing,

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.773
that story with Montana?

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:26.280
- I envisioned myself as my grandmother

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:30.810
and Montana as my mother,
and my mother was only 13.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:35.100
And Mangala split them
up and separated them.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.570
And that was the last place,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.570
that my mother saw her mother get beaten

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.910
and dragged to the gas chambers.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.250
And I looked at Montana and I said,

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.310
no one's ever gonna separate us.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.740
And she put her arm around on me.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.632
We walked arm in arm where
my mother had to walk alone

00:06:53.632 --> 00:06:56.417
and Montana, I got to
walk with my daughter

00:06:56.417 --> 00:06:58.020
and she got to walk with her mother.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.840
And we walked into Auschwitz together,

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:02.540
where my mother had to walk alone.

00:07:03.502 --> 00:07:06.393
- Wow, I'm just gonna need a minute.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.193
- Every time.

00:07:10.193 --> 00:07:12.810
- Humans can be, wow.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:16.623
I mean, we can do beautiful
things, but we can be horrific.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.180
What connections do you
feel between the Holocaust

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.940
and modern day antisemitism?

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.130
What do you feel the connections are?

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:29.823
- Unfortunately, I feel there
are way too many connections.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.780
I visited a camp called Belzec,

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.900
which is a death camp in Poland.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:39.360
Over 500,000 people were murdered there,

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.620
there were only two
survivors from this camp

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:44.280
and they had a small museum there.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:45.390
I went into this museum

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.190
and there was this picture
that really stood with me.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.170
It was a picture of all these Nazis,

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.590
laughing and making fun of
a Jewish man with his beard.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:54.270
And I saw that picture

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:55.650
and that picture really resonated with me,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.980
because we see photos
like that all the time,

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.734
on social media now all
the time, scrolling past,

00:08:01.734 --> 00:08:03.126
you see these types of photos.

00:08:03.126 --> 00:08:04.410
- It's almost like you're desensitized.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:05.243
- Exactly.

00:08:05.243 --> 00:08:06.076
- Yeah.

00:08:06.076 --> 00:08:07.350
- And those photos were sent as postcards,

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.960
back to their family being
like, look what I'm doing.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.610
Which is bizarre, but that's again,

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.260
what's happening nowadays, right?

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:14.093
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:14.926
- With the videos you see surfacing,

00:08:14.926 --> 00:08:16.050
with the photos, you see surfacing.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.320
So people say, "oh the Holocaust
can never happen again",

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:20.460
right, this could never happen again.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.200
How could we allow something
like this to happen again?

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.290
But unfortunately, if we don't
come together as a community,

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:27.480
of all religions, races, ethnicities,

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.810
if we don't come together
and combat anti-Semitism,

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.730
and all forms of hate,
it's gonna happen again.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:34.920
It means unfortunately starting to happen.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:35.753
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:35.753 --> 00:08:37.260
- So that's why this is so important.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:38.093
- Absolutely.

00:08:38.093 --> 00:08:40.616
(crowd applauds)

00:08:40.616 --> 00:08:43.680
Well there's a few simple
things that you can do,

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.260
right now to help spread
awareness and combat hate.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:48.480
The foundation to combat anti-Semitism,

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.570
is launching a new campaign
called Stand Up to Jewish Hate.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.040
They've shared some facts with us,

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.100
that are pretty staggering
that you touched on slightly.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.110
You've probably noticed
that there's a blue square,

00:08:58.110 --> 00:08:59.940
appearing on your screen right now.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.881
It's taking up 2.4% of the screen,

00:09:02.881 --> 00:09:06.000
which actually represents
the entire Jewish population,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.650
in the US, but Jewish
people are victims of 55%,

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.900
of all religious hate
crimes in the country,

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.210
which is horrific.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.850
All of it is horrific, but
like such a small percentage,

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.050
like it's incredible.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:20.820
So you can help raise awareness

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:23.520
by sharing that blue
square emoji in your phone,

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.000
on all social media
platforms to stand up to hate

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.100
and intolerance and just
keep reminding people,

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.180
to never forget.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.599
So thank you so much for
sharing your story with us.

00:09:32.599 --> 00:09:33.432
- [Montana] Thank you so much.

00:09:33.432 --> 00:09:34.530
- It's not easy to like,
share this kind of story,

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.570
with everybody and walk around with that,

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.040
especially with people attacking you,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:39.030
as well as supporting you.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.890
So thank you for doing that.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:42.020
- Thank you, thank you so much.

